#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d766de83-9394-57be-84a4-bb88f5ddf792" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Given in Component Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dc3c841-d079-5e34-8f06-54f8a744419b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{77}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dc3c841-d079-5e34-8f06-54f8a744419b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{77}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256510} :answer-field/id #uuid "eea4ff69-fefd-5dbe-a891-b6cbaf653531" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256509}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0e28f21-d62b-50a2-a5e7-709c0e5d467d" :question/math-academy-id "q-101004" :question/problem "Let $C(3,8,3)$ and $D(-5,0,-6).$ Find $|c+d|,$ where $c$ and $d$ are position vectors of $C$ and $D$, respectively.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=3i+8j+3k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=-5i-6k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates of $c+d$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c+d & =(3i+8j+3k)+(-5i-6k) \\\\  & =-2i+8j-3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|c+d| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{8}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d766de83-9394-57be-84a4-bb88f5ddf792" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Given in Component Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2447bf3-cf3d-5961-a778-5fabc47860c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{170}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2447bf3-cf3d-5961-a778-5fabc47860c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{170}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256513} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b628e84-1b00-593b-802a-bde3fac36696" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256512}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3212fc88-66cd-514c-8c22-3497355c10c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-101008" :question/problem "Let $R(-1,1,4)$ and $S(8,-2,0).$ Find $|3r+s|,$ where $r$ and $s$ are position vectors of $R$ and $S$, respectively.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OR}=r=-i+j+4k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OS}=s=8i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates of $3r+s$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3r+s & =3(-i+j+4k)+(8i-2j) \\\\  & =5i+j+12k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3r+s| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+1+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{170}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1916efa2-6a98-59a5-858c-c4fe64d8e8bc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Given the Coordinates of its Endpoints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff8ce8d9-c2af-5d66-a92b-8af27dd7e627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8ce8d9-c2af-5d66-a92b-8af27dd7e627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256516} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd11dc2f-7a5a-5c9a-82c3-ffed82fec8be" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256515}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e20ebdd4-c7c2-56fa-9bfe-46ac87997401" :question/math-academy-id "q-101019" :question/problem "Let $A(6,7,6)$ and $B(8,9,5).$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{AB}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=6i+7j+6k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=8i+9j+5k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =8i+9j+5k-(6i+7j+6k) \\\\  & =2i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ⟨2,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5856f3cc-5423-5a97-b2fd-7f44e0797ec7" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function With a General Base"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6ece883-5457-512b-817a-6a540f5cafb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6ece883-5457-512b-817a-6a540f5cafb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256531} :answer-field/id #uuid "daadac8d-e9fa-50ed-9624-0e832b2c29a3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256530}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5712e498-177c-5ff5-9270-29807a04505c" :question/math-academy-id "q-101719" :question/problem "Given that $y=7-4{log}_{3}x$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7-4{log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7)-4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =0-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{x\\operatorname{ln}3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3} & ={(-\\frac{4}{x\\operatorname{ln}3})\\,|}_{x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cef7fc21-23f4-5d6a-a208-9963ef23c784" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line of a Logarithmic Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a0d3cd-43bd-5fac-bb10-990cb8b26cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43a0d3cd-43bd-5fac-bb10-990cb8b26cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256534} :answer-field/id #uuid "affa72be-28fd-5889-9af5-8b7396495f2b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256533}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d14b209-f1c5-59b3-ba28-3ccabf73112a" :question/math-academy-id "q-101723" :question/problem "Given that $y=4{e}^{x}-3{log}_{7}x$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=3.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}7}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope  of the tangent at $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(4{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}7})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =4{e}^{3}-\\frac{3}{3\\operatorname{ln}7} \\\\  & =4{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}7}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b933c957-ebae-554d-b937-23a566ec9979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20x}{{(3-{x}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b933c957-ebae-554d-b937-23a566ec9979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20x}{{(3-{x}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256646} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba86529c-b98b-5ba7-ad46-35571f3da4e7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256645}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d411e15-efea-5149-8fe7-10e8ce23e151" :question/math-academy-id "q-104305" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{10}{3-{x}^{2}}$, find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=10$ and $v(x)=3-{x}^{2},$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =0, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot (3-{x}^{2})-(10)\\cdot (-2x)}{{(3-{x}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20x}{{(3-{x}^{2})}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8097de06-6461-5621-941f-80228f745605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+1}{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8097de06-6461-5621-941f-80228f745605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+1}{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256649} :answer-field/id #uuid "19342e98-c7a9-5b5c-803e-795c11b1f081" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256648}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "466a6abc-d194-5c18-a31e-c8516267f1eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-104309" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{3-2x}{2{x}^{2}-2}$, find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=3-2x$ and $v(x)=2{x}^{2}-2,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =-2, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)(2{x}^{2}-2)-(3-2x)(4x)}{{(2{x}^{2}-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{x}^{2}+4-12x+8{x}^{2}}{{(2{x}^{2}-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{4{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+1}{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f97ca5-3776-55ef-9527-31668d5a00d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+1+\\ln2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42f97ca5-3776-55ef-9527-31668d5a00d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+1+\\ln2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256713} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a9136ad-7eb7-53ca-8f13-15c67675376c" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256712}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5abbc5ce-58de-5d2f-bd7e-ce5e196f9c90" :question/math-academy-id "q-107469" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(4-2x)$ at the point $(1,\\operatorname{ln}2).$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}u,\\,u=4-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-2x}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (2-x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={-\\frac{1}{2-x}|}_{x=1}=-\\frac{1}{2-1}=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,\\operatorname{ln}2)$ is $m=-1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\operatorname{ln}2 & =-1(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1+\\operatorname{ln}2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f65f58-bd3d-5c3f-b9e0-462d74d75b59" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d5f8d8b-bf63-5cc8-a242-6b35439d3217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-116" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d5f8d8b-bf63-5cc8-a242-6b35439d3217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-116" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256788} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca84a72e-54ab-56a5-b51a-2a49c1154ad5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256787}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "307089e8-baf9-5482-9f9b-5553f2129d31" :question/math-academy-id "q-10907" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{''}(-1)$ if $f(x)=4{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={(4{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4})}^{'} &  \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4{x}^{4}-4\\cdot 3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =20{x}^{4}-12{x}^{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & ={(20{x}^{4}-12{x}^{3})}^{'} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 20{x}^{3}-3\\cdot 12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =80{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=-1$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(-1) & =80(-1)^{3}-36(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-80-36 \\\\  & =-116.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e88f43c-18c5-5fdb-b6d1-579370489099" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5d1cf60-e8de-5a9d-a628-c991718d7d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5d1cf60-e8de-5a9d-a628-c991718d7d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257181} :answer-field/id #uuid "11d4b53d-4b09-5f75-ac3f-a0cae0b7e836" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257180}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f94e32f6-7fb6-5970-bcb4-34f10163497e" :question/math-academy-id "q-116964" :question/problem "What values is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is in the domain of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76b0ad1e-827e-5795-8431-3276c8eed881" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf497dd6-679d-5131-ac91-2d47cf6c54f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf497dd6-679d-5131-ac91-2d47cf6c54f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257184} :answer-field/id #uuid "7519cd05-eb6f-5c7c-be4e-fa766b0b5e78" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257183}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fd2f5d3-ed41-5722-b4e0-f7436fc89d15" :question/math-academy-id "q-116968" :question/problem "What values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=\\frac{5}{2}$ is not in the range of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ceaa316-e3e1-55b2-ae3c-d9260d2939da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257187} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2f7299-d4e7-5c89-8447-6e85fda04ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257188} {:answer/id #uuid "78468aff-5ac8-572d-a1b3-a73174ef4604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257189} {:answer/id #uuid "16f4a9c6-555a-5cc0-a62a-a0ef7a517e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257190} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7e9d7e-7fd9-5a94-b6f2-b010d988e926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257191} {:answer/id #uuid "36157a81-80f3-5290-8881-297b953400a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257192} {:answer/id #uuid "1e1eb1bf-a535-588b-9c0f-c535e46f7d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257193} {:answer/id #uuid "70eed078-0e2d-5218-b18e-29e7b94f945d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16f4a9c6-555a-5cc0-a62a-a0ef7a517e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257190} :answer-field/id #uuid "6892c31f-549c-572f-9ddb-e3cfa0dc1374" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257186}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c2df21e-7d98-55cc-bb9d-c4c6dd3c0b6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-117039" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nI. - $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nII. - $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$\nIII. - $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$  lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{5\\pi }{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca617c8-ae0c-50ae-9246-ed19fda55c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ca617c8-ae0c-50ae-9246-ed19fda55c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257270} :answer-field/id #uuid "42064ebd-20a9-5478-a842-ced33dee8f82" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257269}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6548405c-a613-5075-b99b-e7774982d1db" :question/math-academy-id "q-118780" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation ${x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}-9{x}^{7}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ which has order $8.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd11e8a-9d28-5792-a51b-1ee774af6cb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257273} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1de0e4-6581-50d8-b4dd-417ce299ef49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257274} {:answer/id #uuid "fd82d032-b6b4-5f9f-bb42-9bc13998c9b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257275} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9a1d31-55dc-5cac-9de6-5d54020933fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257276} {:answer/id #uuid "a3956880-699f-50c3-98e2-75b8a47f798d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257277} {:answer/id #uuid "89b70b1a-c0f9-503a-8b4f-42025621f37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257278} {:answer/id #uuid "a75eaa53-ae4e-5fee-a937-f991724017e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257279} {:answer/id #uuid "81ccf827-073c-5b1e-8402-506130440329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89b70b1a-c0f9-503a-8b4f-42025621f37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257278} :answer-field/id #uuid "257a23f9-a24a-5b27-a803-2fd9ff28766b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257272}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6fe84cb-75e2-55de-b6df-ded3330a421e" :question/math-academy-id "q-118782" :question/problem "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $9x{y}^{''}+8{y}^{'}=4$\nII. - $\\sqrt{{y}^{''}+x}=3y$\nIII. - ${y}^{‴}+{x}^{2}{y}^{''}=3$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\n- Equation II is also a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ee5fc1-03e0-57e5-bbbf-315d46f4703b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257283} {:answer/id #uuid "f57d7578-82c4-5967-a523-96974dbf056c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257284} {:answer/id #uuid "9d8e1b40-531d-5738-bd3c-ddd411adcbb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257285} {:answer/id #uuid "3dac5233-1cb8-538b-a95f-9c3184be17d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257286} {:answer/id #uuid "c9953a88-e46e-55ac-b3c0-ab066edb021f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257287} {:answer/id #uuid "b345a296-774e-57b4-8d0e-503947db627b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257288} {:answer/id #uuid "9d54d347-85b6-56fb-b78d-46ec06bb4b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257289} {:answer/id #uuid "d2471a44-b1ef-5141-9fe5-a87c669720c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dac5233-1cb8-538b-a95f-9c3184be17d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257286} :answer-field/id #uuid "39594d48-3439-593f-beb0-784efbc73181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257282}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d3e1743-cd34-559d-bad0-d34093686fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-118786" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. - $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{'}=8y,\\,y(-1)=3$\nII. - $8{y}^{'}-xy=3$\nIII. - $3{y}^{''}=4y,\\,y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{'}(-1)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{'}=8y$ together with the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $3{y}^{''}=4y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{'}(-1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce3c28-857b-5538-b362-cf52526e3688" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum Containing a Natural Logarithm Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8b3d32-5a1c-5867-b4c3-7f179546897c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}-6x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b8b3d32-5a1c-5867-b4c3-7f179546897c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}-6x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257323} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad78b57a-f611-5b1d-8314-fee4bd97b9e0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257322}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6efa523e-e3d2-57fc-85c3-4bc45ebc82f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-11914" :question/problem "Given that $y=5\\operatorname{ln}x-3{x}^{2}+2x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\operatorname{ln}x-3{x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{x})-3\\cdot 2x+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{x}-6x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f975e5c-637e-57f6-a193-68254799e79b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Sum of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42142022-6da5-5db6-96e0-957c33de6428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42142022-6da5-5db6-96e0-957c33de6428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257607} :answer-field/id #uuid "83199529-9873-5d3d-9f07-29285488bb05" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257606}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c0a953b-56c6-5668-952a-c90e6b1ea708" :question/math-academy-id "q-124064" :question/problem "If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=-\\frac{3}{4}$, \nthen calculate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{a}_{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the constant factor outside of the summation sign gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{a}_{n} & =8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "facb6c88-0c98-5f97-a0bf-1fe64a18c96f" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Finite Number of Terms From a Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e27595-0a81-5428-9abb-089c83c08a05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257613} {:answer/id #uuid "9345163d-02cc-5438-87af-72f9c7ee5893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257614} {:answer/id #uuid "1c66f9e1-7093-5248-babb-26644ce91839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257615} {:answer/id #uuid "3f944afb-cc01-513f-8688-1027dbd1a9e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257616} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ce0565-dbc7-52a4-b169-d172edd975cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257617} {:answer/id #uuid "33220375-c9f2-51c0-b090-41092480c806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257618} {:answer/id #uuid "442a40ce-f652-5e35-8b50-d1e22420cb13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257619} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec4cb49-cedd-56c8-9b45-dc0e15e2bbd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ec4cb49-cedd-56c8-9b45-dc0e15e2bbd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257620} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d0d90d-66d3-54f5-a712-29e05a119d40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257612}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f70941eb-1b43-57e6-9bb7-b412bbea5880" :question/math-academy-id "q-124085" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, which of the following statements is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n},$ where $k$ is a positive integer, and ${a}_{n}$ is well-defined for all $n⩾1?$\n\nI. - It is convergent for $1<k\\le 500$\nII. - It is divergent for $k>500$\nIII. - It is convergent for $50<k\\le 100$" :question/worked-solution "Removing the first $k-1$ terms from a divergent series results in a divergent series.\n\nMore precisely, assume ${a}_{n}$ is well-defined for $n\\ge 1$ and $k\\ge 1$ is an integer. Then, we have the following:\n\n*If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also divergent.*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ead28b84-1f92-510d-87b3-07eb9a394622" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series with Integer Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9223958e-a313-563a-b01a-410817ff2f1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9223958e-a313-563a-b01a-410817ff2f1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257762} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5648d9c-3272-5b32-a9f7-d1439002ed53" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257761}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cf29daa-b0e5-53b9-baa2-725e5b094a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125241" :question/problem "Use the $p$-test to determine whether the series below converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{{n}^{9}}\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n18{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{9}}.\n$$\n\nThis is a $p$-series with $p=9.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent.\n\nNote that the factor of $18$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4dff2ea-a90b-5618-82fc-647a40be0c46" :knowledge-point/title "Convergence Parameters for p-Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82d171de-c05b-58a0-88e7-3bdf0b6ccb39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q<\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82d171de-c05b-58a0-88e7-3bdf0b6ccb39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q<\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257801} :answer-field/id #uuid "b97c1c41-6d2f-53b6-8803-763609ddca4c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257800}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20934715-7c64-505f-835c-e42219a4ae63" :question/math-academy-id "q-125404" :question/problem "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2-3q}}$ converge?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "A $p$-series is any series that takes the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{p}},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. The $p$-series test states that\n\n- if $p>1,$ then the series converges, while\n\n- if $p\\le 1,$ then the series diverges.\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=2-3q.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\n2-3q>1\\,⟹\\,q<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for all $q<\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9368b4e-a855-5fab-b074-a3a505c7ab0c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of Sine or Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b378bd0a-7b2a-59c0-b5a3-7a1f694c97a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b378bd0a-7b2a-59c0-b5a3-7a1f694c97a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257930} :answer-field/id #uuid "03906845-5b9e-5247-b7d2-a1ef31e58f50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257929}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6346f18-6a05-5cb3-99b6-ef873ed75441" :question/math-academy-id "q-133321" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fbbd792-e05f-5d77-acab-60a061084f05" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d13a6b0-e0fd-57b9-9a8d-db15584f6682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d13a6b0-e0fd-57b9-9a8d-db15584f6682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257933} :answer-field/id #uuid "1953e4c1-cae1-5fbb-be88-88080280f26b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257932}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78cb0bb2-b627-5822-b1ef-7eee7e62fe2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-133322" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/72a0459ee5db59562a19961d0304785fb8d46db8be24ad7839d4b8690b0dc835.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases to $-\\infty $, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-6$ and $y=6$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3})=DNE.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fec231d-3911-55db-99af-5e3656333afc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Rate of Change Over a Varying Interval where the Parameter Represents Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62dc3b45-a815-59c2-b2dc-522571722f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62dc3b45-a815-59c2-b2dc-522571722f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257936} :answer-field/id #uuid "be226e8c-bccd-5fef-9814-1659f67fd685" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257935}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15d329ae-5740-515c-97ca-1733812dca77" :question/math-academy-id "q-133323" :question/problem "What is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [3\\pi ,3\\pi +h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n$\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the function $f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}).$ Notice that we are given the length $h$ of the interval. So, the average rate of change of $f$ over this interval is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwith $a=3\\pi .$ Substituting into the formula and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3\\pi +h)-f(3\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{1}{3}(3\\pi +h))-6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})-6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})-6(0)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2b72a2-c486-5a7a-a65e-57e83b78f6cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Limit at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93e8b85a-cbcf-55fd-b21d-724a4d35c312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93e8b85a-cbcf-55fd-b21d-724a4d35c312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257939} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4c911e3-9ea8-5569-8799-f7127763fe8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c776c27-a6a4-5d10-97a0-9fb241329ba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-133324" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1186087a359b3065400101c7f436c3e885bf937477bdd9f73fbdaab6771aa48a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3}).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph shows that as we approach $h=0$ from the left, the $y$ value approaches $-2.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(h)=-2.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $h=0$ from the right, the $y$ value approaches $-2.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(h)=-2.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-2,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59b65fa-d395-5ef3-9a28-1c74301c8104" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of a Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f1be0dd-6f4a-5f0d-8efe-3d4f02a58b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f1be0dd-6f4a-5f0d-8efe-3d4f02a58b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257942} :answer-field/id #uuid "775c24c3-fadf-52a7-91fb-f17a6c72d16f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257941}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "578e3510-260f-5bdc-b7f3-7223ae9ac6f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-133325" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3})}=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/42869c56604cbec85b5e4915f77b42e72444b7d9d117e140d9eba56b58c0f131.png)\n\nFrom the graph, as $x$ approaches $3\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{x}{3})$ are positive and increase without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}={lim}_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{x}{3})=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2072b915-15f9-5296-96dd-707e38df3d10" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With One Quadratic Term by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039afb05-3dd1-55c1-8034-3ea673ee4a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1/4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "039afb05-3dd1-55c1-8034-3ea673ee4a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1/4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257945} :answer-field/id #uuid "209517ec-e06e-50a2-b3b4-440b13e55c97" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257944}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c3c9278-79a7-5543-b4fd-fdc095dcc49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-133326" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{2-2}{(2)^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{1}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5feb914d-efad-5d89-85a9-db99befc9928" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f4ed6e-501b-5b5e-9497-a5e6e518ce62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25f4ed6e-501b-5b5e-9497-a5e6e518ce62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257948} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e480ad3-4c1f-58fa-acab-2ec945f606c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257947}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8555738f-0375-5e38-b16f-5d036c583f49" :question/math-academy-id "q-133327" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3})$" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the product rule to the original limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}[\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot f(x)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10100e89-21d6-53c5-a7de-f14fd73ce4b7" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Different Rules to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e6cb36b-ea61-5db1-94ea-22b111e20e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{(2\\pi +3)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e6cb36b-ea61-5db1-94ea-22b111e20e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{(2\\pi +3)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257951} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f031406-cc55-59c4-9a75-ffc70113c109" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257950}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41f31c41-bebf-52ce-b67c-8ed460afd869" :question/math-academy-id "q-133328" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}f(x)}{(2x+3)^{2}}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}f(x)}{(2x+3)^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}f(x)}{(2x+3)^{2}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\sqrt{3}f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(2x+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n* applying the constant multiple rule in the numerator, and \n\n* the root rule, followed by the sum rule in the denominator, \n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\sqrt{3}f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(2x+3)^{2}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)}{{({lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(2x+3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (3\\sqrt{3})}{(2\\pi +3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{(2\\pi +3)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b5c61a4-3c02-5f45-a6e7-97cf79923e9a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Partial Sums of an Infinite Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "214cea84-cf05-523f-bebb-a3215775a2d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "214cea84-cf05-523f-bebb-a3215775a2d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257954} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca9b11ac-3c21-557c-a64c-fda1dd8a8667" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257953}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b891d1c-e8f1-5953-9447-191cffc40004" :question/math-academy-id "q-133329" :question/problem "Find the third partial sum ${s}_{3}$ of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{f(6n\\pi +\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{n}}.$\n\n${s}_{3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by calculating the first three terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{f(6\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{1}}=\\frac{f(7\\pi )}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{3})}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}=3 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{f(6\\pi \\cdot 2+\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=\\frac{f(13\\pi )}{3}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{13\\pi }{3})}{3}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{2\\cdot 3}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{f(6\\pi \\cdot 3+\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{3}}=\\frac{f(19\\pi )}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{19\\pi }{3})}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+{a}_{3} \\\\  & =3+\\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\  & =4+\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55b978ec-9ec6-5349-a103-0c11d9a904c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Expressed Using Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbf4fa24-4330-573f-9a8d-bbd104e021f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbf4fa24-4330-573f-9a8d-bbd104e021f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257957} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a7c3b23-e791-5048-9fbd-09cfcdbc92e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257956}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cacbbb3f-0a1d-5dc5-9d6e-88b149a9ccf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-133330" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{f(6n\\pi +\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{n}}$ is an infinite geometric series, compute its sum.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we evaluated the first few terms of the series:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=\\sqrt{3},\\,{a}_{3}=1\n$$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=3,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1-(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{\\sqrt{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d98bcee-d473-53b1-a6ef-1943d68702c3" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "711957cc-ce02-5118-a39d-951a94ba97db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\in\\mathbb R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "711957cc-ce02-5118-a39d-951a94ba97db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\in\\mathbb R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258406} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63b139f-bdb4-5136-a43d-b002027fd993" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258405}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7176fbd8-b534-5d23-a9d2-78aa316ce007" :question/math-academy-id "q-156157" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, determine all real values of $K$ for which ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(1+{K}^{2})\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is divergent.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we multiply the terms of a divergent series by a non-zero constant, the resulting series is divergent.\n\nHowever, the expression $1+{K}^{2}$ is always positive.\n\nTherefore, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(1+{K}^{2})\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\nis divergent for all $K.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "06ede1c9-b17d-504b-86a8-9284f7ded093" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros and Asymptotes of a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2a3223c-8742-53bd-8709-f6c1553158e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2a3223c-8742-53bd-8709-f6c1553158e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258420} :answer-field/id #uuid "989bc62c-f1d9-568f-95a9-4796196afea2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258419}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc9adfeb-d883-541f-9bc6-0ae368d34338" :question/math-academy-id "q-156739" :question/problem "What is a horizontal asymptote of $y={log}_{8}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No horizontal asymptotes\"." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{8}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/c9bc40573836e39bfb5c9ea0dab41b66ba7bb753bc7580936a3eb206b968e5c1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note that for $x>1$, the graph grows very slowly as we increase $x.$ \n\nWe say that $y\\to \\infty $ (slowly) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, there are no horizontal asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "06ede1c9-b17d-504b-86a8-9284f7ded093" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros and Asymptotes of a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a943c892-3b5f-5717-b297-77d4278d417c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a943c892-3b5f-5717-b297-77d4278d417c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258423} :answer-field/id #uuid "a156b458-fd69-567e-be85-623822c9cfeb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258422}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0452204-fefa-5ed6-b9ea-ca7949b58b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-156741" :question/problem "What is a horizontal asymptote of $y={log}_{6}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No horizontal asymptotes\"." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{6}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4177d9520b3758b29926eba7a7a74ed4c508d861e3c343f46c372ec79500fc9d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note that for $x>1$, the graph grows very slowly as we increase $x.$ \n\nWe say that $y\\to \\infty $ (slowly) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, there are no horizontal asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f1fce29-a071-5a86-8a5e-0e7093558d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c449a5c-9956-5c45-bb3f-1595038ff2e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258426} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa5e593-c292-526d-965e-73cca1ddab4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258427} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7e53ce-1bbd-56fe-958e-a730dc197ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258428} {:answer/id #uuid "eaba9b1d-0fae-50cc-8ec8-d77adfa6a6e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258429} {:answer/id #uuid "e9be5aba-533d-57b5-a4e7-92b37a03a4c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258430} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd04498-e892-5b43-8f3d-83b52b4c10c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258431} {:answer/id #uuid "55e0dc77-c9f0-5ab9-8fb0-0a905ab4b185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258432} {:answer/id #uuid "97f00078-2a9c-54b8-9d9b-3822a0e5f9af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9be5aba-533d-57b5-a4e7-92b37a03a4c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258430} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dc1a05f-0f59-55a8-ba28-ee76724d625c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258425}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fde27731-67b5-58df-9931-b7a1dd7d4277" :question/math-academy-id "q-156743" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={log}_{6}x?$ \n\nI. - It has no zeros\nII. - $y\\to 6$ as $x\\to \\infty $\nIII. - It has a vertical asymptote at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y={log}_{6}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d8d3f9dc995d8801d451b15129860362efa772178cdc3ef07be5adb6a1da5c45.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has a zero at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As $x$ increases, $y$ increases without bound. Therefore, $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea24c558-8664-5b22-bdfc-71189a45a360" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "306f128e-ca46-571a-befc-b5970976a647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\in\\mathbb R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "306f128e-ca46-571a-befc-b5970976a647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\in\\mathbb R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258436} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9aee00e-02ff-5d24-86cc-1060b058c9b1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258435}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c07bd47-618d-5265-96d8-88410bba9f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-156868" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, determine all real values of $K$ for which $(K+1)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series. \n\nSo, $(K+1)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent for all $K.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea24c558-8664-5b22-bdfc-71189a45a360" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b62fec7-7af2-56b1-b3ac-d6212ff3b694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\in\\mathbb R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b62fec7-7af2-56b1-b3ac-d6212ff3b694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\in\\mathbb R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258439} :answer-field/id #uuid "e81740b9-d305-5523-b1e7-d2891654280e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258438}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a93d0f2e-8dd2-5d9e-9eb1-2f2c0dc6b52a" :question/math-academy-id "q-156873" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, determine all real values of $K$ for which ${K}^{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series. \n\nSo, ${K}^{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent for all $K.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d98bcee-d473-53b1-a6ef-1943d68702c3" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f081fa25-f9ba-5f97-b23b-48406f2126a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f081fa25-f9ba-5f97-b23b-48406f2126a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258448} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2d6360e-374c-5656-a211-5ddc29753536" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258447}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6515e93f-5048-550d-a93c-1295e9ffd539" :question/math-academy-id "q-157059" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent,  determine all real values of $K$ for which  ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{K}{2}-1)\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is **convergent.**\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we multiply the terms of a divergent series by a non-zero constant, the resulting series is divergent.\n\nHowever, multiplying the terms of a divergent series by $0$ gives a series that converges to $0.$\n\nTherefore, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{K}{2}-1)\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\nis convergent when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K}{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{K}{2} & =1 \\\\ K & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d74dd4ee-45f1-5ca7-ae6c-9cbb768ac636" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Finite Number of Terms From a Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fa0482b-bb1b-5a8f-8260-7563e97283f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258451} {:answer/id #uuid "7d42eba7-2ff3-503d-b95d-6b7eb6cdb43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d42eba7-2ff3-503d-b95d-6b7eb6cdb43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258452} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1e69e2-802e-563b-bdb3-25096f2cfd9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258450}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11a1973e-aa91-5290-99cf-1bd17e28a67e" :question/math-academy-id "q-157060" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=85}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}?$" :question/worked-solution "Removing the first $k-1$ terms from a convergent series results in a convergent series.\n\nMore precisely, assume that $k\\ge 1$ is an integer. Then, we have the following:\n\n*If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.*\n\nSo, since ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, we have that ${\\sum }_{n=85}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d74dd4ee-45f1-5ca7-ae6c-9cbb768ac636" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Finite Number of Terms From a Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9db9ea89-a83c-5057-b773-9aa10be347d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258455} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9a4799-774c-58ae-8944-640de36765c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f9a4799-774c-58ae-8944-640de36765c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258456} :answer-field/id #uuid "eae1f70e-1856-5bde-9d2c-039fc97bbfad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258454}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7099c532-a199-562f-8bf0-7842daffe958" :question/math-academy-id "q-157061" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=500}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}?$" :question/worked-solution "Removing the first $k-1$ terms from a convergent series results in a convergent series.\n\nMore precisely, assume that $k\\ge 1$ is an integer. Then, we have the following:\n\n*If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.*\n\nSo, since ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, we have that ${\\sum }_{n=500}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "facb6c88-0c98-5f97-a0bf-1fe64a18c96f" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Finite Number of Terms From a Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57bd7a51-284e-5b66-b11f-cde3b70a46a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258459} {:answer/id #uuid "df193af6-a205-5fbc-9010-7c149cf0304f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258460} {:answer/id #uuid "dacc62f4-43bc-5b7b-a941-026a75dd859f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258461} {:answer/id #uuid "a9501a14-d84d-5471-8838-5b058134ca7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258462} {:answer/id #uuid "b7165fbb-94ce-504d-8e8d-a5797ebea533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258463} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9a7bbc-faa3-53ad-bb90-5b769f46612e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258464} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e26266-1ff5-5e4b-972f-023b84d46d65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258465} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed63383-30c3-5004-be22-85e4846c9bb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed63383-30c3-5004-be22-85e4846c9bb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258466} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cba780f-da49-5d1d-9545-cc0c4c913504" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258458}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b4cfd7c-15a9-5ca2-973b-6cc59e5854a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-157090" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, which of the following statements are true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n},$ where $k$ is a positive integer, and ${a}_{n}$ is well-defined for all $n⩾1?$\n\nI. - It is convergent for $k>{5}^{10}$\nII. - It is divergent for all $k$\nIII. - It is convergent for $1<k<5$" :question/worked-solution "Removing the first $k-1$ terms from a divergent series results in a divergent series.\n\nMore precisely, assume ${a}_{n}$ is well-defined for $n\\ge 1$ and $k\\ge 1$ is an integer. Then, we have the following:\n\n*If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also divergent.*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d138d2c-3295-5eae-990d-42711f5792f1" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing Statements Involving Sums of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ea9fca0-4897-576a-9e90-41e67fec2d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258469} {:answer/id #uuid "1c3b439e-9540-52af-b360-880aebdf9032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258470} {:answer/id #uuid "27d3ed78-69b0-52c2-b26a-ef0c5368e7a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258471} {:answer/id #uuid "ab01aefc-b3ed-5899-adb9-4a261f91b63d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258472} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbe684c-081d-531b-a9d2-ba2ed90e8114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258473} {:answer/id #uuid "75e9cf3f-f7dc-5c31-ae95-fb4f095f918d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258474} {:answer/id #uuid "83a52302-fc8b-5002-949a-5a96afd0d276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258475} {:answer/id #uuid "914e56c2-8ca9-5a35-a730-9b53b61a5911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83a52302-fc8b-5002-949a-5a96afd0d276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258475} :answer-field/id #uuid "c14dff20-ab2a-5d26-a861-fddb4c40b0ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258468}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21799ec1-c57d-5a61-9e39-d42822f17b26" :question/math-academy-id "q-157093" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent\nII. - $12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}=K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is false. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement III is false. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d138d2c-3295-5eae-990d-42711f5792f1" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing Statements Involving Sums of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90b6a9cd-a484-529a-8eae-d44c92b7b4c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258479} {:answer/id #uuid "655f0d00-2dac-5a33-bc59-177ca995b0a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258480} {:answer/id #uuid "7666f9cc-0edd-5246-9564-e920e7845834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258481} {:answer/id #uuid "dea017a7-0a8a-5608-9813-d66b7640c96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258482} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad48b5d-91d0-5691-b2c6-c66e8b6cf618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258483} {:answer/id #uuid "81bedfb0-99b6-5821-9374-1cda5e2e1598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258484} {:answer/id #uuid "44f5addc-8dff-537c-abb2-d9cbb1c80671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258485} {:answer/id #uuid "47cf5ddd-aac8-54a6-9cf6-8ff9e25b3283" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90b6a9cd-a484-529a-8eae-d44c92b7b4c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258479} :answer-field/id #uuid "8be80c42-2a5c-53e9-a30e-081f4ccc8721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258478}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "294c68f1-5d11-5151-93f2-31f75aac6241" :question/math-academy-id "q-157096" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent\nII. - $-9\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=15}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}=K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement III is true. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d138d2c-3295-5eae-990d-42711f5792f1" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing Statements Involving Sums of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e030cded-26f1-5e3b-9d46-4d301b12cffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258489} {:answer/id #uuid "08133c87-65a8-5b63-8453-50e84304f73f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258490} {:answer/id #uuid "cdadc521-ad65-5b9d-8732-d61558d541aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258491} {:answer/id #uuid "53bf4a29-7ee0-57cf-98cc-37448d24a7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258492} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8fcaf8-586a-5faf-bfcb-cfd46f3c322c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258493} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9d461c-c1c4-51d0-a190-93261b2f1a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258494} {:answer/id #uuid "ed84ca34-b06b-5dab-9bff-913989c60b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258495} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1fa0df-840a-5edd-95b9-8a1d0ed49477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e030cded-26f1-5e3b-9d46-4d301b12cffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258489} :answer-field/id #uuid "97c7de96-9ab6-5150-97bd-53fbe2330f76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258488}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e9d21a6-895c-56ef-b36c-707b55ffc68e" :question/math-academy-id "q-157099" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $11\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=16}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent\nIII. - $11\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=16}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}=K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is true. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement III is true. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18b8b49-3de3-54ba-8b4d-ca3c8a50c2c6" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing Statements Involving Sums of Convergent and Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22494098-8b65-5fa2-b2be-110fdcb3be8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258499} {:answer/id #uuid "121bb038-9e31-5a1d-b52a-6d4ad07f1776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258500} {:answer/id #uuid "70607e51-2474-5ba3-a376-fa8ab6de1919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258501} {:answer/id #uuid "967558d8-749b-573a-9452-5b2811814032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258502} {:answer/id #uuid "3753ca99-00a1-54cc-aba0-1684376b462e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258503} {:answer/id #uuid "1af3e68b-7ab7-5b91-aa8f-bc70efb6cd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258504} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2380d0-c509-58f8-a973-f7d08f0df798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258505} {:answer/id #uuid "ab973a9d-f335-5266-ab60-179071c03b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22494098-8b65-5fa2-b2be-110fdcb3be8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258499} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad3a4e2-a944-5a9b-86c6-00785b7a28c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258498}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f21f9a92-e265-5a42-858b-c3523a61288f" :question/math-academy-id "q-157136" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that ${b}_{n}$ is well-defined for $n⩾1.$\n\nI. - $12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent\nII. - $12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{b}_{n}$ is convergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of divergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is also divergent for $K\\ne 0.$\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Any non-zero multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is true. The sum of a convergent series and a divergent series is divergent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Note that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{b}_{n}$ is a **finite series.** Therefore, ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c88884a-128a-5d46-bfbe-3773139ee11c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Years to Yield a Particular Amount"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36592917-0e89-59ea-b1c9-ec6fac2a0b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36592917-0e89-59ea-b1c9-ec6fac2a0b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258613} :answer-field/id #uuid "0158252f-0174-5ffd-92f6-829d16529201" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258612}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c960aee5-3c8c-5166-a2ed-4e95f0b23d9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-166947" :question/problem "If you deposit $$12,000$ into an account with a $8%$ annual interest rate, compounded quarterly, how many years will it take until there is at least $$18,000$ in the account?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded quarterly, and there are $4$ quarters in one year, so $n=4.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=12,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.08.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 18,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ 12,000{(1+\\frac{0.08}{4})}^{4t} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ (1+0.02)^{4t} & \\ge \\frac{18,000}{12,000} \\\\ (1.02)^{4t} & \\ge 1.5 \\\\ log\\,({1.02}^{4t}) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4tlog\\,(1.02) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4t & \\ge \\frac{log\\,(1.5)}{log\\,(1.02)} \\\\ 4t & \\ge 20.475… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{20.475…}{4}\\approx 5.12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember, payments are compounded quarterly, so we must choose either $5$ or $5.25$ years.\n\n- After $5$ years, there is still less than $$18,000$ in the account, because $5<5.12.$\n\n- After $5.25$ years, there will be more than $$18,000$ in the account, since $5.25>5.12.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $5.25$ years ($5$ years and $1$ quarter) until there is at least $$18,000$ in the account."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f65f58-bd3d-5c3f-b9e0-462d74d75b59" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5f5dedb-d7a1-543c-95dd-30eb2991f8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72x+5\\operatorname{sin}x-4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5f5dedb-d7a1-543c-95dd-30eb2991f8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72x+5\\operatorname{sin}x-4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258761} :answer-field/id #uuid "beb3d33d-5499-5dae-98ba-1cc96b74dff5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258760}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0bfad45-7f5d-5462-bf0e-7422f149d562" :question/math-academy-id "q-1852" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{''}(x)$ for $f(x)=12{x}^{3}-5\\operatorname{sin}x+4\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={(12{x}^{3}-5\\operatorname{sin}x+4\\operatorname{cos}x)}^{'} &  \\\\  & =3\\cdot 12{x}^{2}-5(\\operatorname{cos}x)+4(-\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =36{x}^{2}-5\\operatorname{cos}x-4\\operatorname{sin}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & ={(36{x}^{2}-5\\operatorname{cos}x-4\\operatorname{sin}x)}^{'} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 36x-5(-\\operatorname{sin}x)-4(\\operatorname{cos}x) \\\\  & =72x+5\\operatorname{sin}x-4\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6afb4058-908a-5983-9c1e-78ee3fbadf44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives Using Leibnitz Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01539f50-14e5-55b4-b9c0-f017053f2d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}x+30x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01539f50-14e5-55b4-b9c0-f017053f2d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}x+30x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258767} :answer-field/id #uuid "a363de9f-6798-5ba7-b250-28d1adf26f4d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258766}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9bb1b98-cc66-5ed7-8fea-7a253386cbb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-1873" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}x+5{x}^{3}-19.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}x+5{x}^{3}-19) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}x+3\\cdot 5{x}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}x+15{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{cos}x+15{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3(-\\operatorname{sin}x)+2\\cdot 15x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}x+30x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f42eb9ad-cd67-5669-a3d9-b464810319e5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Amount of Money After Compound Interest Is Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8672b7f-fc28-517f-9390-fef8d791e9e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,809" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8672b7f-fc28-517f-9390-fef8d791e9e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,809" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259076} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae70e0b-aec0-57fb-8d87-4b26c4d067e6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259075}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2127f05-8274-5b94-ada9-a82d39e1fd3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-26115" :question/problem "Mike has deposited $$5,000$ into an account paying $1%$ annual interest, compounded monthly. How much money will be in his account after $15$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded monthly, and there are $12$ months in one year, so $n=12.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=5,000,$ an annual interest of $r=0.01,$ and a length of $t=15$ years. Substituting these values into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\  & =5,000{(1+\\frac{0.01}{12})}^{12(15)} \\\\  & =5,000(1+0.000\\,833…)^{180} \\\\  & =5,000(1.000\\,833…)^{180} \\\\  & =5,000(1.161\\,76…) \\\\  & \\approx 5,809.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, after $15$ years, Mike will have $$5,809$ in the account."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f42eb9ad-cd67-5669-a3d9-b464810319e5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Amount of Money After Compound Interest Is Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78b7337f-dfdc-5ac3-96b5-30b075b3aa60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "117,327" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78b7337f-dfdc-5ac3-96b5-30b075b3aa60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "117,327" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259079} :answer-field/id #uuid "f67cfe38-d2bb-5645-a160-78f3670b186a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259078}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbc7d88e-dd0c-51cd-a26d-e7a5e1d7b81a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26182" :question/problem "A principal of $$102,000$ is invested in the stock market and earns interest at a rate of $2%$ per year, compounded daily. To the nearest dollar, how much will the investment with worth after $7$ years (ignore the leap years)?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded daily, and there are $365$ days in one year, so $n=365.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=102,000,$ an annual interest of $r=0.02,$ and a length of $t=7$ years. Substituting these values into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\  & =102,000{(1+\\frac{0.02}{365})}^{365(7)} \\\\  & =102,000(1+0.000\\,054…)^{2,555} \\\\  & =102,000(1.000\\,054…)^{2,555} \\\\  & =102,000(1.150…) \\\\  & \\approx 117,327.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, after $7$ years, the value will be  $$117,327.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e04791d-e117-5a87-8491-1b2b5797f13a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Initial Deposit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c776329f-0851-515c-8a0a-ca4dd2384aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31,323" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c776329f-0851-515c-8a0a-ca4dd2384aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31,323" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259082} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f8fb09e-669f-5ecf-809f-fe87806d984a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259081}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "070b70b8-07a9-553e-acd0-2c4fadb162ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-26198" :question/problem "A savings account offers a $3%$ annual interest rate compounded daily. If the account currently has a balance of $$37,500,$ how much was in the account $6$ years ago (ignore the leap years)? Round your answer to the nearest dollar.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded daily, and there are $365$ days in one year, so $n=365.$ We also have an annual rate of $r=0.03,$ and a final amount of $P=37,500$ after $t=6$ years. To find the initial deposit, ${P}_{0},$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for ${P}_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\ 37,500 & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{0.03}{365})}^{365(6)} \\\\ 37,500 & ={P}_{0}(1.000\\,082…)^{2,190} \\\\ 37,500 & ={P}_{0}(1.197…) \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{37,500}{1.197…} \\\\ {P}_{0} & \\approx 31,323\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $6$ years ago, the savings account had an initial value of $$31,323.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83423c03-d314-5f78-9acf-58cbb6f7774b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d5a123-4fb6-521a-bcac-bb274ffdc3d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09d5a123-4fb6-521a-bcac-bb274ffdc3d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259277} :answer-field/id #uuid "c38b5a73-a839-59b5-814e-abb5f625b64d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259276} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9b0dcd3-9de6-5cc0-ba38-d69ec3de0ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9b0dcd3-9de6-5cc0-ba38-d69ec3de0ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259279} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6d5b760-60d5-5012-bfa5-8667189279b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259278}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bd03f4e-4cf4-5f9e-99a0-8445e7cf14c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-281598" :question/problem "Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $v.$\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $u=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,v\\,| & =\\sqrt{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $v$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83423c03-d314-5f78-9acf-58cbb6f7774b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd6a2755-76c0-560b-8aec-2a7c46d85ca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{65}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd6a2755-76c0-560b-8aec-2a7c46d85ca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{65}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259282} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e02b4ab-23c6-5efe-a930-f01f07fd748f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259281} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eada3912-a2e9-5c0b-b04e-ba218d303a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{65}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eada3912-a2e9-5c0b-b04e-ba218d303a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{65}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259284} :answer-field/id #uuid "95498013-eef1-5685-805c-80ad36dd854e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74cf4bef-d896-5bbd-b1b1-0cf31a04a44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-281604" :question/problem "Given that $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $w.$\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{65}}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $u=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{w}{|\\,w\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,w\\,| & =\\sqrt{{w}_{x}^{2}+{w}_{y}^{2}+{w}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $w$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{w}{|\\,w\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{65}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{65}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{65}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1916efa2-6a98-59a5-858c-c4fe64d8e8bc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Given the Coordinates of its Endpoints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5225462e-ca00-5ed8-a0f7-7975997fdf05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{59}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5225462e-ca00-5ed8-a0f7-7975997fdf05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{59}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259287} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a1c15f-128f-5942-80b0-932025fc61d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259286}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a3261c4-18b3-5911-b162-0718243641dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-281692" :question/problem "Let $R(0,6,-2)$ and $S(-5,1,1).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{SR}|.$\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{SR}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =r=6j-2k, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{OS} & =s=-5i+j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{SR}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}-\\overset{\\to }{OS} \\\\  & =r-s \\\\  & =(6j-2k)-(-5i+j+k) \\\\  & =5i+5j-3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{SR}| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{5}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+25+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{59}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "322168bb-52e3-5352-a2e3-c5bdbf2bc13e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Higher Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aa92c0b-20de-5413-9cd6-e2eed743df17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9aa92c0b-20de-5413-9cd6-e2eed743df17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259300} :answer-field/id #uuid "66cffc73-4a43-5edd-b66b-ade39f8bc170" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259299}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5dd3c3b-72ac-507f-a820-ce546a04182e" :question/math-academy-id "q-28551" :question/problem "Given $y=7{x}^{2}-1+\\frac{1}{2x}$, find $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =7{x}^{2}-1+\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\  & =7{x}^{2}-1+\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{2}-1+\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{2}) \\\\  & =7(2)x-0+\\frac{(-1){x}^{-2}}{2} \\\\  & =14x-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(14x-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}) \\\\  & =14-\\frac{(-2){x}^{-3}}{2} \\\\  & =14+{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(14+{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =0+(-3){x}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75455967-ab5b-5fa9-a900-8596d3bd9923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(6x-7)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75455967-ab5b-5fa9-a900-8596d3bd9923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(6x-7)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259402} :answer-field/id #uuid "79303664-97ab-5458-84fe-059bbf54a7cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259401}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63271a66-41d7-583a-8800-b8588316b76a" :question/math-academy-id "q-310148" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={log}_{5}(7-6x),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)={log}_{5}(u),\\,u=7-6x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(5)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(5)}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(7-6x)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(6x-7)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "322168bb-52e3-5352-a2e3-c5bdbf2bc13e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Higher Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f1fcdbc-a080-57f7-ba71-08ea4d2d53d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f1fcdbc-a080-57f7-ba71-08ea4d2d53d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259405} :answer-field/id #uuid "928663e7-ec6e-52d7-bce4-edd3e45dbad7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259404}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6d68558-2aaa-5e27-875e-de85a290b654" :question/math-academy-id "q-310170" :question/problem "Given $g(x)=4{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{2}{x},$ we have\n\n${g}^{‴}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =4{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+6x-2{x}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6x-2{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =8x+6+2{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{''}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x+6+2{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =8-4{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-4{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =12{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c857ebdc-bf0f-5abb-b2fe-658d35af257b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c857ebdc-bf0f-5abb-b2fe-658d35af257b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259411} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9a0a841-49fd-5c99-8a94-f01b93664851" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259410}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d49772e-f3e5-5d1d-a237-9dc33783e3e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-310188" :question/problem "Differentiate ${3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x).$\n\n$({3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'}=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{3}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={3}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({3}^{x})^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(x)+{3}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3))(\\operatorname{sin}(x))+({3}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{3}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fea484c-5b7e-5008-92b6-4a3b92ea163e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fea484c-5b7e-5008-92b6-4a3b92ea163e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259417} :answer-field/id #uuid "a85f47f0-f0c4-5bf2-a577-9d7ce06f80ad" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259416}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c9eb90c-59cb-57bc-b62b-90c5bc3c0020" :question/math-academy-id "q-31226" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tan}x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})({sec}^{2}x)+(\\operatorname{tan}x)(6x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}x+6x\\operatorname{tan}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=\\pi } & =3{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}x+6x\\operatorname{tan}x{|}_{x=\\pi } \\\\  & =3(\\pi )^{2}{sec}^{2}(\\pi )+6(\\pi )\\operatorname{tan}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 1+6\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3{\\pi }^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43184841-49d1-5070-bdf5-0a234bfec24b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43184841-49d1-5070-bdf5-0a234bfec24b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259527} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39ff20e-51e9-56a7-837f-d05eb8d71985" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259526}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "878043a0-10cb-5659-bc30-ca3c5f00ae2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-319402" :question/problem "Differentiate $(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\operatorname{cos}(x)$\n\n$((2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'}=-{sec}^{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=2-\\operatorname{tan}(x)$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(x)+(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =(-{sec}^{2}(x))(\\operatorname{cos}(x))+(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))(-\\operatorname{sin}(x)) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)+(\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "352d9a06-5c86-5ad5-a31c-625b0ff74a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "352d9a06-5c86-5ad5-a31c-625b0ff74a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259530} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9a3a4c7-b18f-5795-8484-c3728f71e3f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259529}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "458f8052-b021-5a86-a091-16ca938dd05f" :question/math-academy-id "q-319421" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{5}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={5}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={({5}^{x})}^{'}\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}{(\\operatorname{sin}(x))}^{'} \\\\  & =({5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5))\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48cf976f-c492-5116-a478-b5c3d499c7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48cf976f-c492-5116-a478-b5c3d499c7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259545} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c4079ac-5969-5352-90bf-234b527c5450" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259544}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd0e54ab-23dd-5fbf-99e4-afe94b73a3d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-319825" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-1)}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{2(0-1)}{2(0)+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2(0)+1) \\\\  & =(-2)+\\operatorname{ln}(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5ff6e9-0b3c-5118-9120-380862efdc1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c5ff6e9-0b3c-5118-9120-380862efdc1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259548} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc5092f-6845-56f1-ac03-ff53f1048789" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259547}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1572c639-23d1-5637-b129-4ba470408d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-319909" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{2x},$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=2x{e}^{2x}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={e}^{2x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{'}{e}^{2x}+{x}^{2}({e}^{2x})^{'} \\\\  & =(2x)({e}^{2x})+({x}^{2})(2{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a71301e9-bc7f-53b9-900e-c3bd37f96b03" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Natural Logarithm Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99a9c5a7-38b1-50a3-99d9-a4c102af76d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99a9c5a7-38b1-50a3-99d9-a4c102af76d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259860} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a308953-a288-52bd-a0ef-9deb75f23b4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21582e9d-02bc-5764-84aa-8f2d1392331f" :question/math-academy-id "q-339941" :question/problem "Given that $y=-11\\operatorname{ln}x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =-11\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =-11\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce3c28-857b-5538-b362-cf52526e3688" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum Containing a Natural Logarithm Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbff73c0-4107-5d2d-80ee-2e309969accb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}+\\pi {x}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbff73c0-4107-5d2d-80ee-2e309969accb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}+\\pi {x}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259868} :answer-field/id #uuid "34024961-12fc-58f9-a76e-e8aa412781dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259867}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "56a1c713-cc3d-50c8-8cb6-4fdd7363b496" :question/math-academy-id "q-339973" :question/problem "Given that $y=2\\operatorname{ln}x+{x}^{\\pi }+1$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}x+{x}^{\\pi }+1) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{x})+\\pi {x}^{\\pi -1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+\\pi {x}^{\\pi -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5856f3cc-5423-5a97-b2fd-7f44e0797ec7" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function With a General Base"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b574247-6baf-54d0-8f4a-cab77cc1c36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b574247-6baf-54d0-8f4a-cab77cc1c36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259871} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee0c8a1e-b0e6-5071-94f7-653e925f1fa1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259870}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8da817ac-8d36-5248-8a56-e3e3305933b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-339989" :question/problem "Given the function $y=4{x}^{2}-6{log}_{3}x,$ find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}-6{log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2x-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =8x-\\frac{6}{x\\operatorname{ln}3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(8x-\\frac{6}{x\\operatorname{ln}3})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =8(1)-\\frac{6}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =8-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5856f3cc-5423-5a97-b2fd-7f44e0797ec7" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function With a General Base"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ab0884e-e31a-5ed4-a2b3-d2fad90cbba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(7)}-\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ab0884e-e31a-5ed4-a2b3-d2fad90cbba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(7)}-\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259874} :answer-field/id #uuid "49b73348-7610-5233-9840-8f3bef55321f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259873}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b0635f8-e7ae-5a1b-959e-70b659ecfb4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-339997" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={log}_{7}x-3\\operatorname{ln}x$, find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{7}x-3\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{7}x)-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(7)}-3(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(7)}-\\frac{3}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cef7fc21-23f4-5d6a-a208-9963ef23c784" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line of a Logarithmic Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdd5f721-f706-5012-82b2-dd84492aa8b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdd5f721-f706-5012-82b2-dd84492aa8b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259877} :answer-field/id #uuid "9364ab94-e028-59cc-bb87-1b9ee5e2b212" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259876}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84b41208-fbbb-5c93-b4f6-118635b12b3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340014" :question/problem "Given the function $y=5{x}^{2}+6\\operatorname{ln}x,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=3$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{2}+6\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2x+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =10x+\\frac{6}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(10x+\\frac{6}{x})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 3+\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =32\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cef7fc21-23f4-5d6a-a208-9963ef23c784" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line of a Logarithmic Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7efa9c47-eb42-5217-9b01-9324dffcf05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}-18\\operatorname{ln}3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7efa9c47-eb42-5217-9b01-9324dffcf05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}-18\\operatorname{ln}3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259880} :answer-field/id #uuid "93c1c169-e1a5-562c-9421-a536a3e6f150" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259879}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c733d8c4-483f-51cd-af63-0208432f9f16" :question/math-academy-id "q-340022" :question/problem "Given the function $y=5\\operatorname{ln}x-2\\cdot {3}^{x},$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\operatorname{ln}x-2\\cdot {3}^{x}) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-2\\cdot {3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}3 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}3\\cdot {3}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(\\frac{5}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}3\\cdot {3}^{x})|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}3\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-18\\operatorname{ln}3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cef7fc21-23f4-5d6a-a208-9963ef23c784" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line of a Logarithmic Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecadcc24-e607-5843-a6b4-9068f95f75d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecadcc24-e607-5843-a6b4-9068f95f75d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259886} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6bbf671-b44c-59c0-9d96-625daffa19cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259885}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2238b6dc-d73b-58d3-b7fe-799bc2a1f221" :question/math-academy-id "q-340109" :question/problem "Given the function $y=-3{e}^{x}+2{log}_{4}x,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{e}^{x}+2{log}_{4}x) \\\\  & =-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{4}x) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{x}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}4} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(-3{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}4})|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2}+\\frac{2}{2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}4} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4931bc9-bc91-5131-a954-bdb1771462eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4931bc9-bc91-5131-a954-bdb1771462eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259889} :answer-field/id #uuid "263f54f4-c409-5d72-ae68-c6f60c986993" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259888}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94125d6a-8fad-58e8-9ce8-c6017b8b43a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-340290" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$, find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=2$ and $v(x)={x}^{2},$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =0, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b4bc067-52c1-585d-8b3e-e01f0fb731fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b4bc067-52c1-585d-8b3e-e01f0fb731fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259895} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5516765-62a1-566d-a1a7-d1e93e58037d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259894}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "876d6c3c-d8bf-5b17-84ad-fecbaeadd41d" :question/math-academy-id "q-340318" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1.$\n\nThe slope at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={e}^{3x}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(x)}^{'}\\cdot {e}^{3x}-x\\cdot {({e}^{3x})}^{'}}{{({e}^{3x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{3x}-x\\cdot 3{e}^{3x}}{{({e}^{3x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}(1-3x)}{{({e}^{3x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3x}{{e}^{3x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}\\,(1) & =\\frac{1-3(1)}{{e}^{3(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at $x=1$ is $-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af269509-92c0-5a82-b828-e8626f8e8eda" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43f21534-dc51-584d-bce2-28232b621df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{sec}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{tan}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43f21534-dc51-584d-bce2-28232b621df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{sec}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{tan}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259898} :answer-field/id #uuid "acd4aa7d-7e27-5239-b341-720c28271e92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259897}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4792b81-53a1-5620-92e3-e7ad1403e4ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-340336" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}x}{{x}^{2}}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\operatorname{tan}x$ and $v={x}^{2}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & ={sec}^{2}x\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot {sec}^{2}x-\\operatorname{tan}x\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x{sec}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{tan}x)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\,x{sec}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{tan}x\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af269509-92c0-5a82-b828-e8626f8e8eda" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a0b4d1e-797e-5ae7-9daa-2d05442e5018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a0b4d1e-797e-5ae7-9daa-2d05442e5018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259901} :answer-field/id #uuid "efa6c8ef-b590-53a3-958a-ec1a9341ec79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259900}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ffdc549-0c35-5d99-abd9-dd981b4eb66f" :question/math-academy-id "q-340340" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x+1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={e}^{x}$ and $v=x+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)\\cdot {e}^{x}-{e}^{x}\\cdot 1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(x+1-1)}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{(x+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fe750a-8a77-5618-a1c1-805db7e6d8ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{30{x}^{5}}{3-5{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30fe750a-8a77-5618-a1c1-805db7e6d8ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{30{x}^{5}}{3-5{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259923} :answer-field/id #uuid "6785800d-0d72-5219-a1cd-161bd26a5334" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259922}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3893bba9-7764-50a8-af7d-ead40b8716aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-341676" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(3-5{x}^{6})$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}u,\\,u=3-5{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-30{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-30{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-5{x}^{6}}\\cdot (-30{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{30{x}^{5}}{3-5{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62d9a6df-a59b-5467-8482-d97d363a14c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62d9a6df-a59b-5467-8482-d97d363a14c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259929} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e6b75f3-2dff-5d03-a672-54edd32423c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259928}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40b99611-d9a2-5783-9644-833a14c4a6de" :question/math-academy-id "q-341688" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}(8x),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\operatorname{log}(u),\\,u=8x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(10)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(10)}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8x\\operatorname{ln}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{8}}{\\cancel{8}x\\operatorname{ln}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6afb4058-908a-5983-9c1e-78ee3fbadf44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives Using Leibnitz Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fddbd78d-618d-59ac-a246-c3c03921c672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fddbd78d-618d-59ac-a246-c3c03921c672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259997} :answer-field/id #uuid "0369b864-2326-59d6-ba80-f0acde852e7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259996}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d1b276d-d31e-5087-ae03-2c3bbfd6a8d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342868" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}.$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-4{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7}}{4}+1-(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 4{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 2{x}^{6}+0+(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot 2{x}^{-5/2} \\\\  & =14{x}^{6}-3{x}^{-5/2} \\\\  & =14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cfe5a9ca-d045-53ae-bc69-fff9dfbdc439" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Partial Sum of an Infinite Arithmetic Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2831a8e-cd46-5bcf-9e6e-8250017aa423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k(2k+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2831a8e-cd46-5bcf-9e6e-8250017aa423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k(2k+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260100} :answer-field/id #uuid "74bd2c09-e5e2-5529-a707-2f3c847d99ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260099}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "386438bb-70e7-5f64-bd73-4f9e34789bb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-349081" :question/problem "Find the $k$th partial sum ${s}_{k}$ of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(4n-1).$ Express the answer in terms of the parameter $k.$\n\n${s}_{k}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=4n-1.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =3 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =7 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=4$ and ${a}_{1}=3.$\n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{k}=4k-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{k} & =\\frac{k}{2}(3+(4k-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}(4k+2) \\\\  & =k(2k+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4dff2ea-a90b-5618-82fc-647a40be0c46" :knowledge-point/title "Convergence Parameters for p-Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e15372f-2cd6-5b4e-bd26-c10465a76a60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e15372f-2cd6-5b4e-bd26-c10465a76a60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260109} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6b64096-01fd-5615-9728-858bd0f7a199" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260108} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e487520-ea7e-523f-8ec4-434ba60d4177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e487520-ea7e-523f-8ec4-434ba60d4177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260111} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb9082c-0c1e-56c4-b330-c485b0848485" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260110}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1af68890-6058-5926-ba93-36a83ef50f1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-349269" :question/problem "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{-7-4q}}$ converge?\n\n$q$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A $p$-series is any series that takes the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{p}},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. The $p$-series test states that\n\n- if $p>1,$ then the series converges, while\n\n- if $p\\le 1,$ then the series diverges.\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=-7-4q.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\n-7-4q>1\\,⟹\\,q<-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for $q<-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5468f23-98bd-52d9-ba21-85e5eea058a9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain and Range of a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a9aca8c-8551-54d8-b548-b466a4d34126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a9aca8c-8551-54d8-b548-b466a4d34126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260513} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc180485-bcef-5fce-9ffe-383cead88d7f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260512}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a14db00-97f9-5ed3-bfa8-49652c2e587c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47777" :question/problem "What's the domain of $y={log}_{3}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{3}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/c1b7274ff6a0887efcaa27bc44db67934d425c0ca83a19fc761a2cccc72436f6.png)\n\nThe domain of $y={log}_{3}x$ is $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ and the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5468f23-98bd-52d9-ba21-85e5eea058a9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain and Range of a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6574fd45-455a-5ce6-8ad8-68abbf105320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6574fd45-455a-5ce6-8ad8-68abbf105320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260516} :answer-field/id #uuid "82c34cd0-ab73-5aef-b587-d37f07c81adf" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260515}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7b6a962-a925-5eb4-8eb3-c093275b7ef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-47778" :question/problem "What's the range of $f(x)={log}_{9}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nUse an interval in words, such as \"All real numbers\"." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{9}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/40664f83ba162a93ed06970798d37798db90b85c21d44a9ed20f338df4e1b4e1.png)\n\nThe domain of $y={log}_{9}x$ is $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ and the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All real numbers.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18b8b49-3de3-54ba-8b4d-ca3c8a50c2c6" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing Statements Involving Sums of Convergent and Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb78b81a-08e0-5883-b8c1-d2b3762bd274" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260639} {:answer/id #uuid "c88b193c-1e36-53f0-bba8-c3b41432dc35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260640} {:answer/id #uuid "2950368e-f30f-54a6-9cbc-e39d2b227801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260641} {:answer/id #uuid "9764d03e-d2a5-51c2-b135-b591e8355b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260642} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf52af7-4622-5274-b8d9-df77922937e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260643} {:answer/id #uuid "4be3504a-0f66-5048-9275-871a324af799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260644} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5027dd-4dbf-56e5-b293-30e24a303a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260645} {:answer/id #uuid "13f34607-fa97-57d2-b597-fa8c4bd337b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9764d03e-d2a5-51c2-b135-b591e8355b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260642} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39958ab-9a75-5986-89db-259aee63a2ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260638}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9f068f64-42c9-5eab-a9dd-69dbbca65fcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-49836" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that ${a}_{n}$ is well-defined for $n⩾1.$\n\nI. - $\\frac{1}{10\\,000}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=10}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of divergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is also divergent for $K\\ne 0.$\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Any non-zero multiple of a divergent series gives a divergent series.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}{a}_{n}$ is a **finite series.** Therefore, ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Removing a finite number of terms from a divergent series gives a divergent series.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6051f35-c390-57ba-8448-40f53f11c1c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Series Converge From a List"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e82e0a94-0ef1-5341-a309-036af807e925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260671} {:answer/id #uuid "d17dd7b5-7079-5e55-a247-27b2c0ea73e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260672} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7b6a8d-bcfe-50b3-a209-f5009fafc7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260673} {:answer/id #uuid "b0008a49-cef9-5b3e-8b9e-c5f691191a5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260674} {:answer/id #uuid "0d405ad9-1854-5b2f-ba5e-5a63f59fac5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260675} {:answer/id #uuid "233a6e40-88e2-5eb9-86a7-69a846a8e298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260676} {:answer/id #uuid "66bff3b0-0197-5440-8606-f7cf19285068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260677} {:answer/id #uuid "4a02105c-bb09-5f55-963a-149b9d550997" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0008a49-cef9-5b3e-8b9e-c5f691191a5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260674} :answer-field/id #uuid "f719330c-8cad-5dd6-8388-518d937a28d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260670}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a897110c-16a9-5e34-8f8f-61673b309ea9" :question/math-academy-id "q-50629" :question/problem "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[9]{{n}^{2}}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}{2{n}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}.$ Rewriting this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\cdot {n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[9]{{n}^{2}}}=8\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2/9}},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{2}{9}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}{2{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76b0ad1e-827e-5795-8431-3276c8eed881" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6a62d6e-6c68-55b1-88a2-3471115fbcbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6a62d6e-6c68-55b1-88a2-3471115fbcbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260883} :answer-field/id #uuid "85b839e8-884d-5819-b690-473e3370b79b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260882}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ba20a3d-72d2-5250-a2dc-c7c364bd7072" :question/math-academy-id "q-65808" :question/problem "What values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is not in the range of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Given Two of Its Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba2240c-2cbb-5aaf-b814-9c23b8e87e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ba2240c-2cbb-5aaf-b814-9c23b8e87e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261036} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a617679-97a1-5278-90f7-755630e05862" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261035}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd3f6efc-8ad1-5ea4-8f11-4ecf2a1e15c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-67275" :question/problem "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $100$ and a third term equal to $4.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=100$ and ${a}_{3}=4.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{4}{100} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f1fce29-a071-5a86-8a5e-0e7093558d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75311fce-4feb-567e-b92c-49d719962b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261114} {:answer/id #uuid "c402868e-47ed-50be-b9a7-189ca376d7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261115} {:answer/id #uuid "77a8eadb-a898-5c1c-ab62-f63855a24e95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261116} {:answer/id #uuid "40e36838-8b21-54b1-8762-5ad5ccff12d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261117} {:answer/id #uuid "d660b1d0-8f74-5e2a-95a1-a3d4bfbb381a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261118} {:answer/id #uuid "722f8723-5569-5bf5-9c17-5140e64ebe19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261119} {:answer/id #uuid "f7bd8e97-4a84-5829-9d91-27806062c05c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261120} {:answer/id #uuid "5162764c-a93f-5b9a-bd1e-2e9c26b3a693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d660b1d0-8f74-5e2a-95a1-a3d4bfbb381a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261118} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b376239-9277-5a5a-b27c-bad904bb69b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261113}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22db072b-47e0-5531-be05-96bd3329aa8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-69791" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={log}_{5}x?$\n\nI. - The domain is $x>1$\nII. - It has a maximum at $x=1$\nIII. - It passes through the point $(25,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y={log}_{5}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fd8c4765fa1b07d58a4d0b2e90a07383f5475883a2ada747ea6ae32e7a73808b.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, we see that the domain is $x>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain. Therefore, there are no maximum values.\n\n- Statement III is true. Substituting $x=25$ into the equation of the curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{5}(25) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}({5}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c88884a-128a-5d46-bfbe-3773139ee11c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Years to Yield a Particular Amount"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b700da5e-ba2d-5a12-ab05-324fb4f0bdd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b700da5e-ba2d-5a12-ab05-324fb4f0bdd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261157} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fc3f2cd-a3d6-5ac2-af86-a346aff71d9e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261156}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cb756b6f-c9b7-51dc-82e2-024fa8b2534a" :question/math-academy-id "q-70210" :question/problem "If you deposit $$2,000$ into an account with a $20%$ annual interest rate, compounded semi-annually, how many years will it take until there is at least $$4,000$ in the account?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded semi-annually, so $n=2.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=2,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.2.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 4,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 4,000 \\\\ 2,000{(1+\\frac{0.2}{2})}^{2t} & \\ge 4,000 \\\\ (1+0.1)^{2t} & \\ge \\frac{4,000}{2,000} \\\\ (1.1)^{2t} & \\ge 2 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({1.1}^{2t}) & \\ge \\operatorname{log}(2) \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{log}(1.1) & \\ge \\operatorname{log}(2) \\\\ 2t & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{log}(2)}{\\operatorname{log}(1.1)} \\\\ 2t & \\ge 7.272… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{7.272…}{2}\\approx 3.64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember payments are compounded semi-annually, so we must choose either $3.5$ or $4$ years.\n\n- After $3.5$ years, there is still less than $$4,000$ in the account, because $3.5<3.64.$\n\n- After $4$ years, there will be more than $$4,000$ in the account, since $4>3.64.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $4$ years until there is at least $$4,000$ in the account."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e88f43c-18c5-5fdb-b6d1-579370489099" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc44101a-ca73-5339-a70c-a2aa1a8bf5de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc44101a-ca73-5339-a70c-a2aa1a8bf5de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261218} :answer-field/id #uuid "bde52618-fa8a-5788-9d28-4d20315c20d3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261217}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f22b418c-8007-5edd-a5a3-d34d2762b1b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-70620" :question/problem "What values is **not** in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the value $x=\\sqrt{5}$ is not in the domain of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7326080d-cb11-5bc9-9142-38cca1c83229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261221} {:answer/id #uuid "23e40e4a-95c6-5c09-b7ed-eb9094ecabc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261222} {:answer/id #uuid "0399991f-5d77-51c3-b1ad-67ea15f6c800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261223} {:answer/id #uuid "293ad995-8936-53c9-93c7-52ce3ebdf8de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261224} {:answer/id #uuid "08f3c9ef-dfbf-59a9-9a96-c2df57f540a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261225} {:answer/id #uuid "fe618c63-f465-591f-8993-cbc2ddea9f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261226} {:answer/id #uuid "9622d96d-3ab7-5124-85d2-2533e66ade0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261227} {:answer/id #uuid "92f4f470-f57e-5b0f-b9c3-58b694ebfcb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92f4f470-f57e-5b0f-b9c3-58b694ebfcb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261228} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee2c0400-eed5-5e93-a306-0c2d0a11b751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261220}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0a18db1d-9ef4-5e36-9b38-b2a2e715e9c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-70711" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nI. - $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nII. - $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nIII. - $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a71301e9-bc7f-53b9-900e-c3bd37f96b03" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Natural Logarithm Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b522e060-fe6b-5463-838f-6312307330aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b522e060-fe6b-5463-838f-6312307330aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261260} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4834572-d0d9-5af8-9ee4-eb52c2ba9fba" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261259}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ddbc5046-3110-56db-8516-2c66301e420e" :question/math-academy-id "q-70881" :question/problem "Given that $y=2\\operatorname{ln}x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56a4ae0f-dfdf-56d6-b1e1-d59041f87628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56a4ae0f-dfdf-56d6-b1e1-d59041f87628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261300} :answer-field/id #uuid "cef9b926-d5f2-5fb4-a65b-8ba006c8884d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261299}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "568c0119-02eb-5eba-9b08-2993ed865711" :question/math-academy-id "q-71004" :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{sin}x)+({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e04791d-e117-5a87-8491-1b2b5797f13a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Initial Deposit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5256f33b-ca55-58b1-9b5e-750d25ba09c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,946" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5256f33b-ca55-58b1-9b5e-750d25ba09c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,946" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261382} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec202ef-5b97-5714-b8a8-fe4007cba02b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261381}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c0c9df4-4d82-539b-8226-ce3e2837f6e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-72243" :question/problem "How much money would you need to deposit today at a $10%$ annual interest rate, compounded yearly, to have $$40,000$ in the account after $20$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded once per year, so we have $n=1.$ We also have an annual rate of $r=0.1,$ and a final amount of $P=40,000$ after $t=20$ years. To find the initial deposit, ${P}_{0},$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for ${P}_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\ 40,000 & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{0.1}{1})}^{1(20)} \\\\ 40,000 & ={P}_{0}(1.1)^{20} \\\\ 40,000 & ={P}_{0}(6.727…) \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{40,000}{6.727…} \\\\ {P}_{0} & \\approx 5,946\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, you would need to deposit $$5,946.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ead28b84-1f92-510d-87b3-07eb9a394622" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series with Integer Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdf7ece4-2637-5e3a-b02a-bb98b44fc983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdf7ece4-2637-5e3a-b02a-bb98b44fc983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261788} :answer-field/id #uuid "38656401-8add-54f7-bee5-27e9e0e46e34" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261787}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf874b53-ef0a-5fdf-9ef3-0a8f809bc7de" :question/math-academy-id "q-81428" :question/problem "Use the $p$-test to determine whether the series below converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{n}\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nThis is a $p$-series with $p=1$ (the harmonic series). Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\nNote that the factor of $8$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6051f35-c390-57ba-8448-40f53f11c1c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Series Converge From a List"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c79b06e-20b6-545a-bf3f-4ace475d5b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261874} {:answer/id #uuid "8fabf757-29a7-5810-8ad6-bd173bea819e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261875} {:answer/id #uuid "4c35feee-56b7-527a-9eee-d30c114b58ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261876} {:answer/id #uuid "b3815365-f016-5d77-9ce7-512dc1e1c99f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261877} {:answer/id #uuid "cd21ecf6-cc48-56ea-bc16-ba8db7b8c72a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261878} {:answer/id #uuid "906d8a92-c585-5a67-8fad-0f5a15fe64dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261879} {:answer/id #uuid "d22f00a5-7b8e-54b3-958e-6974ad2ff725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261880} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb00b21-f4d3-516a-a25b-c63f9d245980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fabf757-29a7-5810-8ad6-bd173bea819e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261875} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa99ed83-7eac-5c53-a422-b00ad94c2731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261873}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59b096bf-4af9-5e57-941d-caa731cefc46" :question/math-academy-id "q-83593" :question/problem "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3{n}^{5}}$" :question/worked-solution "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n}.$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n}=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=1$ (the harmonic series). Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\cdot {n}^{1/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3{n}^{5}} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=5.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f975e5c-637e-57f6-a193-68254799e79b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Sum of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441dd12d-ad06-54c0-b50d-8bafad595eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "441dd12d-ad06-54c0-b50d-8bafad595eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261979} :answer-field/id #uuid "078bc752-e359-55d9-ac87-b5b28ffbedb8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261978}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "770c1ff4-046b-5d4d-9ce0-c706d161310b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9293" :question/problem "If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=0.5,\\,$ ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}=9$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}=-2,$ then calculate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{b}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{c}_{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the constant factors outside of the summation signs gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{b}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{c}_{n} & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}+8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n} \\\\  & =3(0.5)+2(9)+8(-2) \\\\  & =1.5+18-16 \\\\  & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "855a9c5c-9d01-584b-9b24-99834365b732" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series With Fractional Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce94ff1f-e592-5911-a4d8-01d6f5dd50dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce94ff1f-e592-5911-a4d8-01d6f5dd50dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262003} :answer-field/id #uuid "801ab594-17cd-508c-b3db-45ed4c7ea1d1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262002}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e09514d4-1e05-5fb3-ba5a-f0e56cf060c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-9332" :question/problem "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[8]{{n}^{7}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{7/8}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{7}{8}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "855a9c5c-9d01-584b-9b24-99834365b732" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series With Fractional Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f434f7ee-2d39-5a22-9c70-41bd16dc432f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f434f7ee-2d39-5a22-9c70-41bd16dc432f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262006} :answer-field/id #uuid "bccb7fea-91f1-5488-9cfc-3f870d14529a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262005}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47306eda-69a1-5c4c-86bf-6d6e220f28a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-9334" :question/problem "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Since $p>1$, the series is convergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $3$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af8323ab-ddb1-5f48-a9f7-f5692ae339da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262045} {:answer/id #uuid "f8784524-b57e-5054-9fa6-028c173f4c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262046} {:answer/id #uuid "e07ee293-119a-578f-9cb9-bdb3e5415610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262047} {:answer/id #uuid "d24cd77e-16ed-5f5a-9c5e-0234683e143e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262048} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d72649-3668-5f78-ada6-9565b30f7fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262049} {:answer/id #uuid "dd234ff3-f83e-5cf9-87fe-e14c16f76f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262050} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cfd61a-0a0f-5f14-900b-5550cae3912a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262051} {:answer/id #uuid "06e6c92a-97d1-564d-863d-bda507afccfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06e6c92a-97d1-564d-863d-bda507afccfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262052} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f7fab41-ea7c-53fd-85ac-6541129d2e5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262044}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d484918-7854-547b-b753-257beeb0c7d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96702" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x={e}^{y}$\nII. - $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2=\\sqrt{y}$\nIII. - $4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{x}{y}=5y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{y}+x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={e}^{y}+x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{x}{y} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4}(5y+\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{4}(5y+\\frac{x}{y}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60ed9cbb-4ba4-51aa-a0f8-7225e1688c36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262075} {:answer/id #uuid "31036b5b-7449-5599-8b7f-8b39b778f239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262076} {:answer/id #uuid "be9955de-2d2b-52bd-ad19-a9c11284301d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262077} {:answer/id #uuid "c053f2d6-9364-55b3-954f-91552728a08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262078} {:answer/id #uuid "8163967d-e6d8-56ce-9dca-756d234c3020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262079} {:answer/id #uuid "11bda8cf-ada4-544f-8e86-30b984916eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262080} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a652c4-ff96-5e6d-b026-86d6eed744a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262081} {:answer/id #uuid "9e078445-c2fc-5a31-88ab-c2be941bd0f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8163967d-e6d8-56ce-9dca-756d234c3020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262079} :answer-field/id #uuid "831ff5c2-b840-5fe4-b6ea-ec995d5e6729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262074}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6f5f12c-8742-57e1-8431-b7e5f7c8b33f" :question/math-academy-id "q-96712" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${y}^{'}=1-\\sqrt{y}$\nII. - ${y}^{'}-\\operatorname{tan}y={e}^{2}$\nIII. - $x{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{y}=4$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-\\operatorname{tan}y & ={e}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\operatorname{tan}y+{e}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\operatorname{tan}y+{e}^{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{y} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{4y+1}{xy} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{4y+1}{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "491c18dc-e9d5-5a2b-88f5-a3bc86a065ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262095} {:answer/id #uuid "6cee0851-d168-5175-af4b-4e9ec6593fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262096} {:answer/id #uuid "e20c0aff-74cc-552d-96eb-eef3a81e96ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262097} {:answer/id #uuid "f0377b83-e245-52ea-80a0-298ddfe36899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262098} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bcccdb-85b9-52d9-b7ca-5d988b9f7fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262099} {:answer/id #uuid "070e48b3-a56e-5c40-9343-941d4858140e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262100} {:answer/id #uuid "40d7f3bf-ff28-581c-96a7-f1b98edbccd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262101} {:answer/id #uuid "f1223c20-84d2-5fb5-bf8a-545a08f3d710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "070e48b3-a56e-5c40-9343-941d4858140e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262100} :answer-field/id #uuid "36b80bee-db64-535d-aace-058b279199ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262094}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f324e71f-0765-5434-b640-0e2263e787be" :question/math-academy-id "q-96992" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. - $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\nII. - $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\nIII. - ${y}^{'}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d4fd620-0900-55af-8837-e928f5f4ee8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d4fd620-0900-55af-8837-e928f5f4ee8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262132} :answer-field/id #uuid "150d451a-00ce-5a55-8435-8e6fa936af48" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262131}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1aeb34a-99a8-5372-b85e-e163f31127c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-9908" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}x$ and $v(x)=1+\\operatorname{sin}x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-{sin}^{2}x-{cos}^{2}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-({sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since ${sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-1)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}]}
